      <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">The Story</h2>
      <p>Every leader I've worked for has needed the same thing underneath whatever title they gave me: someone who could take a principal's day and an organization's priorities and make both of them hold, without needing to be watched. I've done that since 2019, under <a href="/hire-me/">thirteen job titles</a>, and the work itself has barely changed.</p>
      <p>It started small. In 2019 I was coordinating a virtual team spread across Nairobi for a fintech lender: scheduling content, building performance reports, tracking who owed what to whom by the end of the day. The tracking process I built to hold that team accountable is, with almost no changes, the one I still run. The pattern was already there before I had a name for it.</p>
      <p>An asset-finance platform is where it became a discipline. Two years running CRM at scale for one of East Africa's best-known names in that space taught me the thing everything since has been built on. Reliability doesn't come from working harder inside a broken process. It comes from rebuilding the process. I reworked ticket tracking until response times held under real volume, and I kept the data clean enough that senior management could actually trust what they were looking at.</p>
      <p>A business process outsourcing firm is where the pattern became irreversible, and where it actually started. I wasn't hired into a finished company. I was brought in on the idea itself, and helped ground it from the floor up, by the side of the CEO, before there was much to point to. That early trust is what everything after was built on. Over the years that followed, the remote workforce grew past 200, team response time dropped 80% after I rebuilt the workflows underneath it, and the SOP library the floor still runs on was mine. Eventually that work became a title: Chief of Staff.</p>
      <p>None of it happened one job at a time, and that ground-floor trust is what carried into the next chapter. A Swedish fintech entering the Kenyan market for the first time needed someone who could handle that kind of exposure before there was a playbook to follow, so for a year the two ran side by side. I was Country Lead and Executive Partner to their Global CPO, keeping a live pipeline of twenty-plus joint-venture and capital-partnership talks with microfinance banks and development finance institutions visible at any hour, to anyone who needed it.</p>
      <p>Right now that split runs in parallel across four organizations. For the Group CEO of a multi-entity auto-services group, I'm Executive Administrator: correspondence, meeting preparation, and the coordination that keeps decisions moving across the parent company and its subsidiaries. For a UK law firm where privilege governs every note I take, I work as fractional Chief of Staff: visibility into the team, the client side, and the partners and agents involved, with direct ownership of shifts and outcomes, not just a summary of them after the fact. For a wellness brand in Kenya, I'm back in the Executive Administrator seat. And for an individual executive coach in the US, I work as Operations Partner, doing much of the same work I do at the law firm, visibility, team ownership, outcomes, except the business is one person instead of an institution, and the coordination has to bridge Nairobi hours with US Eastern time.</p>
      <p>Thirteen titles: operations support, customer operations executive, executive partner, chief of staff, executive administrator, operations partner. Different employers reaching for whatever language their org chart already had. Strip the titles away and there are really only two disciplines underneath: keep a principal's day defensible, and keep a team's outcomes owned rather than just tracked. I've run both, at several organizations at once, for long enough that I stopped thinking of it as multitasking. It's just the job. What I'm offering isn't a title. It's ownership of whichever one your desk is missing right now.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">How I Work</h2>
      <p>Three principles, visible in every engagement. <strong>Visibility:</strong> every commitment tracked, every owner named. Dashboards replace inbox archaeology. <strong>Discipline:</strong> SLAs on response time, decisions logged, prep delivered 24 hours ahead. <strong>Async-first:</strong> documented processes and decision-ready summaries built for multi-time-zone teams. The full methodology is on <a href="/services/">the services page</a>; the receipts are in <a href="/case-studies/">the case studies</a> and <a href="/portfolio/">portfolio</a>.</p>

